1. l-multiplet (K = 1, V = L phi^2/(2 r^2), L = l (l + n - 1)), phi = eps^{1/2} vphi
  [ok] Hamiltonian, order 1:  beta = 4 s vphi_t^2 / c^2 + 2 l vphi^2
  [ok] slicing - Hamiltonian, order 1 (beta eliminated):  c_s = 2 vphi_t^2 / c
  [ok] field equation, order eps^{1/2}:  d_t(vphi_t/c) = c (vphi_s - l vphi/(2 s))
   (the n-dependence is gone: the D = infinity inner problem is n-free for every fixed l)

2. l = 0: the potential form of GAMMA.md's autonomous heat system
  [ok] Y_t = c S
  [ok] S_t = c (Y_s + Y^3/4)   (identity, given c_s = 2 vphi_t^2/c)
  [ok] c_s = c Y^2 / 4

3. wave map (K = eta / n, V = eta sin^2 u / (2 r^2); eta = kappa f^2 / 2 fixed), u = O(1)
  [ok] Hamiltonian, order 1:  beta = 4 eta s u_t^2/c^2 + 2 eta sin^2 u
  [ok] slicing - Hamiltonian, order 1 (beta eliminated):  c_s = 2 eta u_t^2 / c
  [ok] field equation, order eps:  d_t(u_t/c) = c (u_s - sin(2u)/(4 s))
   eta -> infinity with u = vphi / sqrt(eta): sin(2u)/(4s) -> vphi/(2 s sqrt(eta)), c_s -> 2 vphi_t^2/c: the l = 1 multiplet
  [ok] wave map -> l = 1 multiplet as eta -> infinity (field equation)
  [ok] wave map -> l = 1 multiplet as eta -> infinity (c_s)

4. Yang-Mills U-system (struct_eqs.py, section 5): U = eps^{1/2} V
  [ok] Hamiltonian, order 1:  beta = 2 s V_t^2/c^2 + 2 V^2   (l = 1 multiplet at V = sqrt2 vphi: 2 s V_t^2/c^2 + V^2)
  [ok] slicing - Hamiltonian, order 1 (beta eliminated):  c_s = V_t^2 / c   (= 2 vphi_t^2 / c)
  [ok] field equation, order eps^{1/2}:  d_t(V_t/c) = c (V_s - V/(2 s))   (the YM-specific terms are O(eps^{3/2}))
   (the YM-U stress tensor differs from the multiplet's in rho - P_r: 2n(n-1) vs n^2 in struct_eqs.py's
    normalization; in the inner region that difference sits in beta, which first enters the equations at O(eps))

5. Flat self-similar wave-map blowup and its inner limit
  [ok] u0 = 2 arctan(r / (sqrt(n-1)(-t))) solves the flat wave-map equation for every n (d = n + 1 >= 3)
  [ok] u = 2 arctan(sqrt(2 s)/(-t)) solves the flat inner equation u_tt = u_s - sin(2u)/(4 s)
   (x = sqrt(n) xi: 2 arctan(x/sqrt(n-1)) -> 2 arctan(xi), with xi = rho/(-t), rho = sqrt(2 s): the same solution)
   With gravity the CSS ansatz u = U(xi), c = C(xi) is consistent with the inner wave-map system for every eta
  [ok] CSS reduction of the inner wave-map field equation is T-free (an ODE in xi)
  [ok] CSS reduction of c_s = 2 eta u_t^2 / c is T-free

6. Flat Yang-Mills at large n: the inner equation
  [ok] W_tt = W_rr + (n-2) W_r/r + (n-1) W(1-W^2)/r^2  ->  W_tt = W_s + W (1 - W^2)/(2 s) + O(eps)
   With gravity, an O(1) field has (kappa/n) r^2 rho_YM ~ n l_YM^2/(4 r^2) = l_YM^2/(8 s): strong for s <~ l_YM^2,
   i.e. this flat inner problem is never reached at small scales (struct_eqs.py, section 5).

7. An exact static (omega = 0) solution of the real-scalar inner problem (README form)
  [ok] c = (1 - xi^2)^(-1/2), y = sqrt2 xi c, sigma = sqrt2 xi^2 c^2:  L y = c sigma
